  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight through Tuesday night: The band that brought snow to much of \nthe Inland Northwest today is pivoting across the Northeast \nMountains and North Idaho at 200 PM this afternoon. Communities \nalong the Pend Oreille River Valley, around Priest Lake, and up the \nPurcell Trench will see light snow into the early evening. There may \nbe a light dusting that occurs on roads as the sun sets, but not \nmuch impactful snow is expected for the duration of this frontal \nband. In its wake is a nearly saturated boundary layer, and the \nthreat for fog will increase through the evening hours. Fog may \nbecome dense with visibility down to 1/4 mile and less. \nTemperatures are also expected to drop below freezing tonight. \nThis will set up the potential for black ice with foggy \nconditions, which is a bad combination when driving and would \ninclude the Monday morning commute.\n\nWe will be in a break on Monday before a warm front moves across the \nCascades Tuesday into Tuesday night. There is a discrepancy \nbetween the drier GEFS and wetter ECMWF ensemble guidance. The \nECMWF ensemble is just a little further east with a shortwave \nrounding an upper level low pressure system in the eastern \nPacific. That slight eastward placement makes a big difference \nthe potential for snow accumulations over the Cascade passes. \nThe GEFS suggests a potential range of 3 to 6 inches, whereas \nthe ECMWF ensemble mean is suggesting more in the 5 to inches \nrange. The RRFS model guidance forecast around 6 inches at \nStevens Pass and around 10 inches for Washington Pass. Snow \nlevels between 3000 to 3500 feet over the Cascades means that \nSnoqualmie will also see the potential for heavy wet snow. Those\nplanning to travel for the holidays will want to keep a very \nclose eye on the forecast and be prepared for winter travel \nconditions.\n\nWednesday through Sunday: The good news for holiday travelers is \nthat the air mass will modify over the latter half of the week. A \nweak Atmospheric River (AR) is directed toward the Northwest on \nThanksgiving. The bad news is that the prognoses is for a wet \nThanksgiving day, particularly earlier in the day when turkey \ntrotters may be out looking to get some exercise in. Prepare for wet \nconditions if that is the case. The milder air moving with the AR \nwill drive snow levels up to between 4000-4500 feet overnight \nWednesday into Thanksgiving morning. This will continue to \nbring the risk of heavy wet snow over Stevens Pass, and \nWashington Pass indubitably see the potential for heavy snow \nwith over a foot of new snow expected along the North Cascades \nHighway over the pass. Sherman Pass will see the potential for 2\nto 5 inches. Winds pick up a little bit across the exposed \nareas of the Columbia Basin in the afternoon on Thanksgiving. \nExpect south to southwest winds with gusts up to around 30 mph.\n\nThe Northwest then looks to shift toward a split storm track. The \nensemble model guidance suggests moisture primarily being diverted \nsouth of the region with an active sub-tropical jet. Not to say that \nthe Northwest will be dry over next weekend with the probability of \nadditional precipitation being between 30-60%. Snow levels will\nbe relatively high increasing to around 5000 feet and higher \nFriday into Saturday. Snow levels lower a bit to around \n4000-4500 feet Saturday night. Precipitation that we do see is \nexpected to be light and mainly rain the valleys with snow in \nmountains, especially closer to the Canadian border. /SVH",
